{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01EDSEQsVAv5CP7JRRtcLNe7", "type": "tool_result", "content": "The file /srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-001-r1s0/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "572e4ea2-8de4-46f5-b0db-18c8e72b15f7", "uuid": "53a7e3a9-c5e1-4625-8f2a-8da0c17aac7f", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:18:09.878Z", "tool_use_result": {"filePath": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-001-r1s0/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md", "oldString": "| cocotb unit tests (`pytest cores/bench/test`) | 6 files pass: alu 15, decoder 31, divider 13, pipeline 20, imm_gen 8, reg_file 5 |\n\nNot run: `make fpga` (3 placement options + CoreMark IPC) and `make formal-deep`.\nSo the scored median Fmax and the iterations/cycle figure are not measured\nhere; only place option 0 was timed.", "newString": "| cocotb unit tests (`pytest cores/bench/test`) | 6 files pass: alu 15, decoder 31, divider 13, pipeline 20, imm_gen 8, reg_file 5 |\n| CoreMark bracketed cycles (`run_coremark_ipc`, `--bench --istall --dstall`) | 4,491,817 vs 4,491,485 for the baseline RTL built from HEAD: **+332 cycles, +0.007%** |\n\nFmax x iter/cycle on these numbers: 44.49 MHz x 2.2263e-6 = ~99.0 iter/s,\nagainst 5.44 MHz x 2.2264e-6 = ~12.1 iter/s for the baseline (about 8.2x).\n\nNot run: `make fpga` (median over 3 placement options) and `make formal-deep`.\nOnly place option 0 was timed, so the scored median Fmax may differ from the\n44.49 MHz above.", "originalFile": "# hyp-20261123-001 — multi-cycle divider + single 33x33 multiplier\n\n## Result of local checks\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `verilator --lint-only -Wall` (plain and `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`) | clean, 0 warnings |\n| `bash formal/run_all.sh` (nret=1, ALTOPS) | **53 passed, 0 failed**, first attempt (no fix iterations needed) |\n| `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` | **passed**, 2 ELFs (selftest + CoreMark, with and without stalls) |\n| `make timing TARGET=bench` (Gowin, place option 0 only) | **44.49 MHz** (was 5.44), 2400 logic, 634 regs, 2 DSP, 21 logic levels |\n| cocotb unit tests (`pytest cores/bench/test`) | 6 files pass: alu 15, decoder 31, divider 13, pipeline 20, imm_gen 8, reg_file 5 |\n\nNot run: `make fpga` (3 placement options + CoreMark IPC) and `make formal-deep`.\nSo the scored median Fmax and the iterations/cycle figure are not measured\nhere; only place option 0 was timed.\n\nNote on `make cosim`: plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` fails in `cosim-build`\nbefore reaching the comparison, because `test/cosim/build.sh` defaults to\n`NRET=2` and this core is `nret: 1` (main.cpp then references `io_rvfi_*_1`).\nThat is independent of this change; `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` builds\nand passes.\n\n## What changed\n\n- `rtl/divider.sv` (new): sequential radix-2 restoring divider.\n  - Handshake `req` / `ack` / `done`. `req` = valid DIV/REM in ID/EX (held for\n    the whole operation), `ack` = EX/MEM not held by dmem stall, `done` is a\n    dedicated flop that stays high until `ack`.\n  - FSM: IDLE -> PREP (take magnitudes) -> RUN x32 (one 33-bit subtract per\n    cycle on `{rem, quo}`) -> FIX (conditional negate, registered) -> DONE.\n  - Operands and op are sampled every cycle while idle, so the values kept are\n    those of the start cycle (forwarded rs1/rs2). No extra enable logic.\n  - Div-by-zero and INT_MIN/-1 fall out of the datapath: quotient is not\n    negated when the divisor is 0 (all ones survives), remainder takes the\n    dividend's sign.\n  - `!req` or reset always returns the FSM to idle.\n  - Under `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`: same handshake, IDLE -> FIX -> DONE, result\n    `(a - b) ^ const` from the latched operands.\n- `rtl/alu.sv`: three 64x64 multipliers replaced by one signed 33x33\n  (`a_sx`/`b_sx` extension bit per op); DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU arms removed in both\n  the real and the ALTOPS branch (they fall to `default: 0`).\n- `rtl/ex_stage.sv`: instantiates the divider, exports `div_busy`, selects the\n  divider result into `EX/MEM.alu_result`, reports the latched operands as\n  `rs1_val`/`rs2_val` for a divide, writes a bubble (ctrl = 0, valid = 0) into\n  EX/MEM while busy unless a dmem stall holds it.\n- `rtl/hazard_unit.sv`: `div_busy` added to `stall_if` and `stall_id`.\n- `rtl/core_pkg.sv`, `rtl/decoder.sv`, `rtl/id_stage.sv`, `rtl/core.sv`: new\n  `ctrl.is_div` bit (funct7 = 0000001 and funct3[2], inside the already\n  validated R-type arm) and the `div_busy` wire.\n- Tests: `test_divider.py` (new), `test_alu.py`, `test_pipeline.py`,\n  `test_decoder.py` — see below.\n\n## Deviations from the hypothesis plan\n\n1. **Latency is 36 cycles in EX (35 more than before), not ~34-35.** PREP and\n   FIX are each their own registered cycle so that neither the magnitude\n   negate nor the final negate sits behind the forwarding mux or in front of\n   the EX/MEM register. One or two cycles per divide could be bought back by\n   folding them in; I did not, since divides are rare in CoreMark.\n2. **ALTOPS latency is 3 cycles in EX (2 bubbles).** Chosen so that both\n   forwarding sources (EX/MEM, then MEM/WB) have drained by the time the\n   result is taken — formal therefore does exercise the operand latch.\n3. **`flush_id` is unchanged** (no `!div_busy` term). With a divide in ID/EX,\n   `load_use_hazard` needs `ID/EX.mem_read` and `redirect` needs\n   `is_branch`/`is_jump`, so both are 0. Leaving it out keeps the\n   redirect -> flush path, which is the critical one, untouched.\n4. **`write_data` still takes the live `rs2`** for a divide rather than the\n   latched copy. It is only used by stores, so it is don't-care here.\n5. **Bubble clears only `ctrl` and `valid`**, not the whole EX/MEM register;\n   data fields of a bubble are don't-care.\n6. **DIV/REM vectors live in a new `test_divider.py`, not in `test_alu.py`.**\n   The shared `run_cocotb` helper runs every test in a module against one\n   toplevel, and `_helpers.py` is outside the editable set, so a second\n   toplevel needs its own module. `test_alu.py` keeps the combinational ops,\n   gains a Python-model sweep for the four MUL ops (144 corner pairs + 200\n   random each, plus an op-interleaving test), and checks the ALU returns 0\n   for the four divide op codes.\n\n## Tests added\n\n- `test_divider.py`: per op, 256 corner pairs + 60 random vectors against a\n  Python RV32M model, driven through the handshake; operands are scrambled\n  after the start cycle to prove they are latched; `ack` held low for several\n  cycles; back-to-back divides with `req` kept high (also asserts fixed\n  latency); `req` dropped mid-operation.\n- `test_pipeline.py`: divide with both operands forwarded, back-to-back\n  divides feeding each other plus a dependent ADD/MUL, RV32M corner cases\n  incl. `rd = x0`, load -> divide -> store, taken branch on a divide result,\n  and four seeds of divides mixed with loads/stores under ~30% random imem\n  and dmem stalls (`_run` gained an optional `stall_seed`). `divider.sv` was\n  added to the runner's source list.\n- `test_decoder.py`: `is_div` set for exactly DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU and clear for\n  look-alike encodings.\n\n## Concerns\n\n- **Fmax is below the hypothesis' scratch measurement** (44.5 vs 49.4 MHz on\n  place option 0). The worst path (27.8 ns) is the pre-existing one: regfile\n  BSRAM output -> ID/EX bypass -> EX forward mux -> branch compare ->\n  redirect -> IF NOP mux -> hazard -> ID/EX. The divider is not on it, but the\n  forward-mux outputs now also feed the divider's 64 operand flops, and the\n  design is ~430 LUTs larger than the scratch copy, either of which may\n  account for the gap. I did not isolate which.\n- **IPC effect not measured.** Each DIV/REM now costs 35 extra cycles. The\n  claim that CoreMark's timed region executes almost none is the hypothesis'\n  premise; cosim confirms correctness of the run, not the cycle count.\n- **Real divider arithmetic is not covered by the formal gate** (ALTOPS). It\n  rests on `test_divider.py`, the pipeline tests and cosim; `make formal-deep`\n  was not run.\n- The regfile/ID-EX path is now the limiter by a wide margin over everything\n  the divider added, so further Fmax has to come from the branch-resolve /\n  redirect loop, not from this change.\n", "structuredPatch": [{"oldStart": 9, "oldLines": 11, "newStart": 9, "newLines": 15, "lines": [" | `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` | **passed**, 2 ELFs (selftest + CoreMark, with and without stalls) |", " | `make timing TARGET=bench` (Gowin, place option 0 only) | **44.49 MHz** (was 5.44), 2400 logic, 634 regs, 2 DSP, 21 logic levels |", " | cocotb unit tests (`pytest cores/bench/test`) | 6 files pass: alu 15, decoder 31, divider 13, pipeline 20, imm_gen 8, reg_file 5 |", "+| CoreMark bracketed cycles (`run_coremark_ipc`, `--bench --istall --dstall`) | 4,491,817 vs 4,491,485 for the baseline RTL built from HEAD: **+332 cycles, +0.007%** |", " ", "-Not run: `make fpga` (3 placement options + CoreMark IPC) and `make formal-deep`.", "-So the scored median Fmax and the iterations/cycle figure are not measured", "-here; only place option 0 was timed.", "+Fmax x iter/cycle on these numbers: 44.49 MHz x 2.2263e-6 = ~99.0 iter/s,", "+against 5.44 MHz x 2.2264e-6 = ~12.1 iter/s for the baseline (about 8.2x).", " ", "+Not run: `make fpga` (median over 3 placement options) and `make formal-deep`.", "+Only place option 0 was timed, so the scored median Fmax may differ from the", "+44.49 MHz above.", "+", " Note on `make cosim`: plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` fails in `cosim-build`", " before reaching the comparison, because `test/cosim/build.sh` defaults to", " `NRET=2` and this core is `nret: 1` (main.cpp then references `io_rvfi_*_1`)."]}], "userModified": false, "replaceAll": false}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01EDSEQsVAv5CP7JRRtcLNe7", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "mode"}}]}
