{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01Nc7YEi8CyvCkqVfr6ECUvq", "type": "tool_result", "content": "The file /srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-003-r1s2/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "efc18fa1-a986-416f-b405-8ea1312d06ca", "uuid": "d6f9fd61-6477-4f62-aba2-45706bb942d0", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:14:22.264Z", "tool_use_result": {"filePath": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-003-r1s2/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md", "oldString": "- Timing (`make timing TARGET=bench`): TIMING_PLACEHOLDER", "newString": "- Timing (Gowin, `python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options`, and\n  the same flow on the baseline HEAD RTL for a like-for-like comparison):\n\n  | placement option | baseline HEAD | this change |\n  |------------------|---------------|-------------|\n  | 0                | 5.140 MHz     | 5.117 MHz   |\n  | 1                | 5.442 MHz     | 5.564 MHz   |\n  | 2                | 5.442 MHz     | 5.564 MHz   |\n  | **median**       | **5.442 MHz** | **5.564 MHz** |\n\n  The worst path is unchanged in kind: MEM/WB `rd` -> forward_unit ->\n  EX operand mux -> ALU divider -> EX/MEM `alu_result` (absorbed into the\n  bench dmem BSRAM address register), ~200 ns, 149-151 logic levels. No\n  predictor / fetch-steering logic appears in the reported worst paths.\n  The +2.2% median Fmax is placement noise, not a property of the change.\n  Area: logic 12312 -> 12246, registers 460 -> 463, LUT-RAM 8 -> 24 (the\n  16 extra cells are the 256x2 counter table, inferred as distributed RAM\n  as intended), BSRAM/DSP unchanged.\n- Expected fitness from the two numbers above: iterations/cycle x1.071,\n  median Fmax x1.022 (noise) -> roughly +7% from the hypothesis itself,\n  about +9.5% as measured locally.", "originalFile": "# Implementation notes — fetch-cycle branch prediction (bimodal-256 + in-cycle pc+imm target)\n\n## What changed\n\n- `rtl/branch_predictor.sv` (new): 256 x 2-bit saturating-counter table\n  (`IDX_BITS=8`), async read at the fetch PC, sync write from EX, no reset\n  on the array.\n- `rtl/core_pkg.sv`: `if_id_t` and `id_ex_t` gain `pred_taken` (1 bit) and\n  `pred_ctr` (2 bits).\n- `rtl/if_stage.sv`: pre-decodes BRANCH / JAL from `imem_data`, computes\n  `pred_target = pc + (B|J imm)` with one adder, and\n  `pred_taken = !(flush||redirect) && !imm[1] && (is_jal || (is_branch && ctr[1]))`.\n  PC priority: reset > EX redirect > (!stall ? (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4) : hold).\n  Exposes `pc[9:2]` to the predictor; emits `pred_taken` / `pred_ctr` in\n  the IF/ID bundle.\n- `rtl/id_stage.sv`: latches `pred_taken` / `pred_ctr` into ID/EX with the\n  rest of the bundle (cleared on flush, held on stall).\n- `rtl/ex_stage.sv`: misprediction-only redirect:\n  `actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) && !misalign_fault`,\n  `redirect = actual_taken != in.pred_taken`,\n  `redirect_target = actual_taken ? (is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target) : pc+4`.\n  Exports the training port (`in.valid && is_branch`, resolved direction,\n  `in.pc[9:2]`, fetched counter). `pc_next`, trap, link value and the\n  EX/MEM payload are unchanged (the two `in.pc + 4` uses now share one\n  `pc_plus4` wire).\n- `rtl/core.sv`: instantiates the predictor; training strobe is gated with\n  `!stall_ex_mem` so a branch held by a dmem stall trains exactly once.\n- `test/test_branch_predictor.py` (new): 7 cocotb tests for the table.\n- `test/test_pipeline.py`: `branch_predictor.sv` added to the source list;\n  3 new end-to-end tests (predicted loop is architecturally exact and\n  zero-bubble once trained, with exactly one bubble on the mispredicted\n  exit; JAL is zero-bubble with correct link; misaligned JAL/BEQ targets\n  are never steered and still trap + fall through).\n\n`hazard_unit.sv` is untouched: `redirect` now simply means \"EX disagrees\nwith what fetch did\", and the existing flush/stall rules already give the\nneeded invariant — ID/EX captures a valid IF/ID bundle exactly when the PC\nadvances on the prediction (imem stall -> NOP with pred_taken=0 and PC\nheld; load-use -> ID/EX flushed and PC held; dmem stall -> both held;\nredirect -> ID/EX flushed).\n\n## Deviations from the hypothesis\n\n1. **Counter value rides down the pipeline instead of a second read port.**\n   The plan's update port was `(en, pc index, taken)`, which needs the old\n   counter read at the EX index — a second async read port, i.e. a\n   duplicated LUT-RAM (or a fallback to 512 flops). Instead the 2-bit\n   counter read at fetch travels with the branch (`pred_ctr`, 2 FFs in\n   ID/EX) and comes back as `upd_ctr`; the module applies the saturating\n   +/-1 and writes it. The table is then the canonical 1-write / 1-async-\n   read distributed RAM. Behavioural difference: none unless another\n   branch aliasing the same entry trains between this branch's fetch and\n   its EX (only possible for a branch whose immediate predecessor in EX\n   maps to the same index, e.g. a branch-to-self); it still converges.\n2. **Reserved BRANCH funct3 (2/3) is excluded in the pre-decode** (one\n   extra 2-bit compare) so an illegal branch encoding is never steered.\n   Not required for correctness (EX would redirect to pc+4), just avoids a\n   pointless redirect.\n3. `pred_imm` is selected with opcode bit 3 (JAL vs BRANCH) rather than the\n   full `is_jal` compare — identical for the two opcodes that can assert\n   `pred_taken`, a don't-care otherwise.\n4. No `initial` block on the table: Verilator and the FPGA LUT-RAM both\n   start at 0 (strongly not-taken) rather than the trace study's\n   weakly-not-taken; formal sees arbitrary contents. Warm-up cost is one\n   extra mispredict per taken-biased entry — negligible.\n\n## Results (local)\n\n- Lint (`verilator --lint-only -Wall`): clean, no warnings.\n- Formal (`RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`,\n  nret=1 auto-detected): **53 passed, 0 failed** on the first attempt\n  (no fix iterations needed). The table is unconstrained in the proof.\n- Cosim (`NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`): **passed** (selftest with and\n  without stalls, CoreMark CRCs under --istall --dstall).\n- cocotb: `test_branch_predictor.py` 7/7, `test_pipeline.py` 14/14.\n- CoreMark, `--bench --istall --dstall`, bracketed cycles for 10 iterations:\n  baseline HEAD RTL 4,491,485 -> this change 4,193,173 cycles\n  (**-6.64% cycles, +7.11% iterations/cycle**), CRCs\n  0xe9f5 / 0xe714 / 0x1fd7 / 0x8e3a / 0xfcaf, \"Correct operation validated.\"\n- Timing (`make timing TARGET=bench`): TIMING_PLACEHOLDER\n\n## Notes on running the gates\n\n`make formal TARGET=bench` and plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` both fail\nbefore reaching the RTL on this nret=1 core, with or without this change:\nthe Makefile passes `formal/checks.cfg` explicitly (which disables the\nnret auto-detect and selects the dual-channel wrapper), and `cosim-build`\ncompiles `main.cpp` with the default `NRET=2`. The commands above are the\nsingle-issue equivalents (the orchestrator's own gates read `nret` from\n`core.yaml`).\n\n## Concerns\n\n- The scheme relies on the PC always being 4-aligned (so `imm[1]` alone\n  decides target alignment). That holds by induction: reset PC is 0,\n  predicted targets add an immediate with `imm[1:0]=0`, and EX only\n  redirects to aligned targets or pc+4. riscv-formal pc_fwd/pc_bwd pass.\n- New fetch-cycle path: `io_imemData -> imm mux -> 32-bit adder -> PC mux`\n  plus `io_imemReady / redirect -> pred_taken -> PC mux select`. Expected\n  to be far off the divider-dominated critical path; see timing line above.\n", "structuredPatch": [{"oldStart": 77, "oldLines": 8, "newStart": 77, "newLines": 28, "lines": ["   baseline HEAD RTL 4,491,485 -> this change 4,193,173 cycles", "   (**-6.64% cycles, +7.11% iterations/cycle**), CRCs", "   0xe9f5 / 0xe714 / 0x1fd7 / 0x8e3a / 0xfcaf, \"Correct operation validated.\"", "-- Timing (`make timing TARGET=bench`): TIMING_PLACEHOLDER", "+- Timing (Gowin, `python3 -m tools.eval.gowin bench --all-options`, and", "+  the same flow on the baseline HEAD RTL for a like-for-like comparison):", " ", "+  | placement option | baseline HEAD | this change |", "+  |------------------|---------------|-------------|", "+  | 0                | 5.140 MHz     | 5.117 MHz   |", "+  | 1                | 5.442 MHz     | 5.564 MHz   |", "+  | 2                | 5.442 MHz     | 5.564 MHz   |", "+  | **median**       | **5.442 MHz** | **5.564 MHz** |", "+", "+  The worst path is unchanged in kind: MEM/WB `rd` -> forward_unit ->", "+  EX operand mux -> ALU divider -> EX/MEM `alu_result` (absorbed into the", "+  bench dmem BSRAM address register), ~200 ns, 149-151 logic levels. No", "+  predictor / fetch-steering logic appears in the reported worst paths.", "+  The +2.2% median Fmax is placement noise, not a property of the change.", "+  Area: logic 12312 -> 12246, registers 460 -> 463, LUT-RAM 8 -> 24 (the", "+  16 extra cells are the 256x2 counter table, inferred as distributed RAM", "+  as intended), BSRAM/DSP unchanged.", "+- Expected fitness from the two numbers above: iterations/cycle x1.071,", "+  median Fmax x1.022 (noise) -> roughly +7% from the hypothesis itself,", "+  about +9.5% as measured locally.", "+", " ## Notes on running the gates", " ", " `make formal TARGET=bench` and plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` both fail"]}], "userModified": false, "replaceAll": false}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01Nc7YEi8CyvCkqVfr6ECUvq", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "mode"}}]}
