{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_012182P362cAt7SX5AALYZ3j", "type": "tool_result", "content": "===== .agent.hyp-20261123-001-r1s0.log\n154 lines\n--- text:\nThe hypothesis file is written and schema-valid. The first timing probe failed on an import path, so I'm re-running it with the path fixed.\n--- text:\nThe hypothesis is written to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r1s0.yaml` and validates against the schema.\n\n**Proposal (micro_opt):** replace the combinational divider with a multi-cycle iterative one that stalls the pipeline, and collapse the three 64-bit multipliers into one 33×33 signed multiplier that stays single-cycle.\n\n**Why:** the 199.8 ns worst path that pins Fmax at 5.44 MHz runs through the combinational `/` and `%` in `alu.sv`. The proposal assumes CoreMark executes almost no DIV/REM in the timed region, so iterations per cycle should barely move; I did not count them.\n\n**Timing probe:** I ran the Gowin flow (place option 0 only) on a scratch copy of the RTL with the divider arms deleted and the single multiplier in place. This approximates the proposal — it does not include the new divider or its stall logic, and it was not simulated.\n\n| Variant | Fmax | LUTs | DSP |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | 5.44 MHz | 12312 | — |\n| Divider removed, one 33×33 single-cycle multiplier | 49.41 MHz | 1971 | 2 |\n| Divider and multiplier both removed | 46.7 MHz | 1922 | 0 |\n\n- **Multiplier is not the next bottleneck:** removing it as well does not raise Fmax, so the hypothesis keeps MUL single-cycle and loses no CoreMark throughput.\n- **Remaining worst path:** about 25.5 ns, from the regfile through EX branch resolve, redirect and the hazard unit into the ID/EX register. This change does not touch it.\n- **Expected gain:** roughly 9× Fmax if the probe holds once the real divider is in. The YAML states `fitness_delta_pct: 50` with high confidence because 50 is the schema ceiling.\n\nThe YAML also specifies how the divider should latch forwarded operands, interact with dmem stalls, and behave under `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` (same handshake, short fixed latency) so the formal checks still close.\n\nThe w\n…[5519 of 8591 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:56248e84f9c6c88f]…\nt combinational multipliers are replaced by one 33×33 signed multiply with registered operands and product.\n- **Branch path:** redirect currently feeds the fetch-stage NOP mux and then the load-use compare. Redirect and load-use can never fire together, so the hypothesis removes redirect from that mux.\n\nThings the implementer must get right, all spelled out in the YAML:\n- **Stale operands:** while an M-op waits in EX its producers retire, so ID/EX operand values must be refreshed with the forwarded values on every held cycle.\n- **Formal window:** under `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` the M-unit keeps the same handshake but uses a fixed 2-cycle latency, because a 34-cycle divide cannot retire inside the 20-step check depth.\n- **Test coverage:** every M-extension vector in `test_alu.py` moves unchanged to a new `test_muldiv.py`, so invariant 5b coverage is preserved.\n\nOne side effect I did not check: `make formal-deep` (no ALTOPS, outside the orchestrator loop) may no longer reach a real 34-cycle divide within its depth.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ae9a54ea-9e8c-4880-9286-5c199a6b23a5", "uuid": "43aee2e1-722f-4464-a3be-7630280b611d", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:20:18.538Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "===== .agent.hyp-20261123-001-r1s0.log\n154 lines\n--- text:\nThe hypothesis file is written and schema-valid. The first timing probe failed on an import path, so I'm re-running it with the path fixed.\n--- text:\nThe hypothesis is written to `cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-001-r1s0.yaml` and validates against the schema.\n\n**Proposal (micro_opt):** replace the combinational divider with a multi-cycle iterative one th