{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChLeBurwBEuPyqdgYX7qH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BnhqTNyPgXxgiP21MuU5BJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd cores/bench && f=LESSONS.md && before=$(wc -l < \"$f\") && if [ -s \"$f\" ] && [ -n \"$(tail -c1 \"$f\")\" ]; then printf '\\n' >> \"$f\"; fi && cat >> \"$f\" <<'EOF'\n- 2026-11-23 hyp-20261123-002-r1s1 (improvement, +1039.44%): Moving `*`, `/`, `%` out of alu.sv into a stalled multi-cycle muldiv.sv (ex_busy holds PC and ID/EX, EX/MEM takes bubbles) won entirely on clock period — Fmax 5.44 -> 63.3 MHz, 12312 -> 2381 LUT4, 6 -> 2 DSP — for only ~2% more CoreMark cycles (derived from fitness/Fmax, ~2.18 CoreMark/MHz), because the timed region runs almost no divides and few multiplies, so M-op latency is nearly free and MUL needs only DSP input registers (1 extra cycle; operand reg -> DSP -> EX/MEM was not in the top-5 paths of the implementer's single-seed timing run, so the product register was dropped); the design passed formal/cosim with a fixed 1-extra-cycle ALTOPS variant of the same FSM (a 35-cycle divide cannot retire inside the 20-step BMC) and with post-forward rs1/rs2 written back into ID/EX on every held cycle because the waiting op's producers drain out of EX/MEM and MEM/WB (the unrefreshed case was not tried), which is the template for any future op that waits in EX; the M-unit is now off the critical path, which sits at ~21-22 ns in two co-critical single-cycle EX loops (regfile -> operand mux -> branch compare -> redirect -> ID/EX flush, ~6 ns shorter than the sibling's 27.8 ns version that still ran through the if_stage NOP mux and hazard_unit load-use compare, a cross-design comparison; and MEM/WB.rd -> forward_unit -> ALU add -> dmem BSRAM address), so the next gains must come from branch resolution and forwarding, not from tuning the M-unit.\nEOF\necho \"lines: $before -> $(wc -l < \"$f\")\"; tail -c 300 \"$f\"; git st