{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChLeCwHYMJ4RbEv2pdU8n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I appended one bullet for `hyp-20261123-002-r1s1` to `cores/bench/LESSONS.md`; the earlier bullet is preserved and no other file was touched.\n\nThe lesson records three things, without repeating the sibling's entry:\n\n- **Why it won:** the gain is all clock period (Fmax 5.44 → 63.3 MHz, 12312 → 2381 LUT4) for about 2% more CoreMark cycles, because the timed region runs almost no divides and few multiplies. MUL needs only DSP input registers, one extra cycle.\n- **Template for any op that waits in EX:** a fixed short-latency ALTOPS variant of the same FSM (a 35-cycle divide cannot retire inside the 20-step BMC), and post-forward rs1/rs2 written back into ID/EX on every held cycle.\n- **Next limiter:** the M-unit is off the critical path, which now sits at about 21–22 ns in two co-critical EX loops — branch compare → redirect → ID/EX flush, and forward → ALU add → dmem address.\n\nCaveats on the evidence, all flagged in the bullet itself:\n\n- The ~2% cycle cost is derived from fitness ÷ Fmax, not read from a logged cycle count.\n- The critical-path detail comes from the implementer's single-seed timing runs.\n- The ~6 ns saving on the redirect path is a comparison against the sibling design, not an isolated measurement.\n- The design was never run without the operand write-back, so its necessity is reasoned, not demonstrated."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1730,"cache_read_input_tokens":71481,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1730},"output_tokens":516,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d55f0dbd-27d2-4ad1-ae4e-feea