{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01S5ki4qmEnjhGk6FfJV3fTw", "type": "tool_result", "content": "===== baseline-bench-0b45336\n  id: baseline-bench-0b45336\n  title: Baseline retest for cores/bench\n  category: micro_opt\n  outcome: improvement\n  fitness: 12.12\n  delta_pct: 0.0\n  vs_baseline: 0.0\n  fmax_mhz: 5.442\n  ipc_coremark: 2e-06\n  cycles: 4491485\n  iterations: 10\n  lut4: 12312\n  ff: 460\n  seeds: [5.14, 5.442, 5.442]\n  perturbations: [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]]\n  lutram: 8\n  bsram: 6\n  dsp: 6\n  critical_path: {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.542, \"arrival_ns\": 199.837, \"logic_cells\": 156, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n  logic_levels: 150\n  formal_passed: true\n  cosim_passed: true\n  error: null\n  implementation_notes: \n  timestamp: 2026-11-23T22:00:47.001716Z\n  round_id: 0\n  slot: 0\n===== hyp-20261123-001-r1s0\n  id: hyp-20261123-001-r1s0\n  title: Multi-cycle iterative divider + single 33x33 multiplier: take DIV/REM off the EX critical path\n  category: micro_opt\n  motivation: Fmax is 5.44 MHz because the worst path (199.8 ns, 156 cells) runs\nforward_unit -> ex_stage operand mux -> alu -> EX/MEM register through the\npurely combinational 32-bit `/` and `%` operators in alu.sv (four of them:\nsigned/unsigned quotient and remainder). Everything else in the 5-stage\npipeline is an order of magnitude shorter. CoreMark executes almost no\nDIV/REM instructions, so the whole clock period is being set by an\noperation that is practically never used. The same alu.sv also builds three\nseparate 64x64-bit multipliers (mul_ss, mul_uu, mul_su with 64-bit\nsign-extended operands) where one 33x33 signed multiplier is sufficient;\nonce the divider is gone those become the next-longest EX path and they\nalso waste DSP blocks / LUTs (12.3k LUT4 today vs. a 10k target).\n\n  hypothesis: {\"motivation\": \"Fmax is 5.44 MHz because the worst path (199.8 ns, 156 cells) runs\\nforward_unit -> ex_stage operand mux -> alu -> EX/MEM register through the\\npurely combinational 32-bit `/` and `%` operators in alu.sv (four of them:\n…[11005 of 14077 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8e88aeb1d9642998]…\n extra cycle; operand reg -> DSP -> EX/MEM was not in the top-5 paths of the implementer's single-seed timing run, so the product register was dropped); the design passed formal/cosim with a fixed 1-extra-cycle ALTOPS variant of the same FSM (a 35-cycle divide cannot retire inside the 20-step BMC) and with post-forward rs1/rs2 written back into ID/EX on every held cycle because the waiting op's producers drain out of EX/MEM and MEM/WB (the unrefreshed case was not tried), which is the template for any future op that waits in EX; the M-unit is now off the critical path, which sits at ~21-22 ns in two co-critical single-cycle EX loops (regfile -> operand mux -> branch compare -> redirect -> ID/EX flush, ~6 ns shorter than the sibling's 27.8 ns version that still ran through the if_stage NOP mux and hazard_unit load-use compare, a cross-design comparison; and MEM/WB.rd -> forward_unit -> ALU add -> dmem BSRAM address), so the next gains must come from branch resolution and forwarding, not from tuning the M-unit.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "56a9f7e4-f52e-40a5-a6f8-de4bf29d2f35", "uuid": "d0830155-6a6c-45f7-ba94-98710144f404", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:23:51.726Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "===== baseline-bench-0b45336\n  id: baseline-bench-0b45336\n  title: Baseline retest for cores/bench\n  category: micro_opt\n  outcome: improvement\n  fitness: 12.12\n  delta_pct: 0.0\n  vs_baseline: 0.0\n  fmax_mhz: 5.442\n  ipc_coremark: 2e-06\n  cycles: 4491485\n  iterations: 10\n  lut4: 12312\n  ff: 460\n  seeds: [5.14, 5.442, 5.442]\n  perturbations: [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2,