{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01EjfSCWuR1t2LrxB7oMdGnA", "type": "tool_result", "content": "68\t[221] A-TEXT: Cosim passes (selftest with/without stalls + CoreMark CRCs). Measuring the CoreMark cycle count while formal runs.\n69\t[222] A-TOOL Bash: sed -n 66,100p tools/eval/cosim.py; ls cores/bench/obj_dir/cosim_sim && (cores/bench/obj_dir/cosim_sim bench/programs/coremark.elf --bench --istall --dstall 2>&1 | tail -15)\n70\t[223] RESULT:     except Exception as e: ⏎         return {'passed': False, 'elf': elf.name, 'field': 'error', 'detail': str(e)} ⏎  ⏎  ⏎ def run_coremark_crc(coremark_elf: Path, sim_bin: str, worktree: str, env: dict | None = None) -> dict: ⏎     \"\"\"Run coremark.elf on the sim and validate UART-reported CRC. ⏎  ⏎     Full-trace cosim would take >30 min; the CRC guard is sensitive to any ⏎     computational bug since CoreMark's CRC is computed over every algorithm's ⏎     working set. This is the same validation run_fpga_eval applies and ⏎     cross-checks it at the cosim stage too. ⏎     \"\"\" ⏎     # Late import so fpga.py's asyncio/statistics deps aren't required for ⏎     # projects that only run cosim. ⏎     from tools.eval.fpga import validate_coremark_uart, parse_iterations ⏎     # Stall flags match the orchestrator's fitness eval so the CRC validation ⏎     # gate exercises the same workload that fpga.py scores. See ⏎     # tools/eval/fpga.py:COREMARK_SIM_FLAGS for the rationale. ⏎     try: ⏎         result = run_pgroup( ⏎             [sim_bin, str(coremark_elf), \"50000000\", ⏎              \"--bench\", \"--istall\", \"--dstall\"], ⏎             capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=600, ⏎             cwd=Path(worktree).resolve(), env=env ⏎         ) ⏎     except subprocess.TimeoutExpired: ⏎         return {'passed': False, 'elf': coremark_elf.name, 'field': 'timeout'} ⏎     except Exception as e: ⏎         return {'passed': False, 'elf': coremark_elf.name, 'field': 'error', 'detail': str(e)} ⏎  ⏎     # rc==3 means ebreak reached but the CPU made a\n71\t[224] A-TOOL Bash: cores/bench/obj_dir/cosim_sim bench/programs/coremark.elf 50000000 --bench --istall --dstall 2>&1 | tail -5 | cut -c1-90\n…[32202 of 35274 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9f1bfd3a71ae0f34]…\n branch is never steered.\n168\t- **Table start state:** there is no `initial` block, so counters start at 0 rather than weakly-not-taken.\n169\t\n170\t`make formal TARGET=bench` and plain `make cosim TARGET=bench` fail on this nret=1 core before reaching the RTL, because they select the dual-channel wrapper and `NRET=2`. I ran the single-issue equivalents instead: `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` and `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`.\n171\t\n172\tNotes are in `cores/bench/implementation_notes.md` (the gitignored path) rather than the worktree root, where the file would have shown up as untracked. Only `cores/bench/rtl/` and `cores/bench/test/test_*.py` show in `git status`.\n173\t[439] result: {\"duration_api_ms\": 435995, \"stop_reason\": \"end_turn\", \"session_id\": \"efc18fa1-a986-416f-b405-8ea1312d06ca\", \"total_cost_usd\": 4.545664200000001, \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 62, \"cache_creation_input_tokens\": 132405, \"cache_read_input_tokens\": 3224841, \"output_tokens\": 44543, \"output_tokens_details\": "}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "56a9f7e4-f52e-40a5-a6f8-de4bf29d2f35", "uuid": "443a2374-05a7-40de-a511-d1bdaf4a1941", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:24:37.355Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/srv/hwebench/tmp/06925bfd/06a322b3/claude-1003/impl003.txt", "content": "[221] A-TEXT: Cosim passes (selftest with/without stalls + CoreMark CRCs). Measuring the CoreMark cycle count while formal runs.\n[222] A-TOOL Bash: sed -n 66,100p tools/eval/cosim.py; ls cores/bench/obj_dir/cosim_sim && (cores/bench/obj_dir/cosim_sim bench/programs/coremark.elf --bench --istall --dstall 2>&1 | tail -15)\n[223] R