{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-fable-5-2", "id": "msg_011ChLceKBdGZhqmLq7m9G1o", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01PWKb5euCbS1UrJTjPuqrAP", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-002-r1s1-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-002-r1s1.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20261123-002-r1s1\ntitle: \"Multi-cycle M-unit: iterative divider + registered DSP multiplier behind an EX-stall handshake\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Fmax is 5.44 MHz because the worst path (199.8 ns, 150 logic levels) runs\n  MEM/WB.rd -> forward_unit -> EX operand mux -> alu.sv combinational 32-bit\n  `/` and `%` -> EX/MEM.alu_result (= dmem BSRAM address). The single-cycle\n  dividers are also why the core is 12.3k LUT4 with only 460 FFs. Three\n  separate 64-bit combinational multipliers (mul_ss/mul_uu/mul_su, 6 DSPs)\n  sit in the same forward -> ALU -> EX/MEM loop and are the next limiter once\n  the divider is gone. CoreMark's timed region executes essentially no\n  divides and only a few percent multiplies, so making these ops multi-cycle\n  costs ~1-3% cycles (CoreMark/MHz stays ~2.15-2.2) while the clock period\n  can drop by an order of magnitude. Nothing else in the design is within\n  10x of this path, so every other optimisation is invisible until this\n  structural change lands.\nhypothesis: |\n  Take multiply/divide out of the single-cycle EX path. A new sequential\n  M-unit (rtl/muldiv.sv) executes them over several cycles while the M\n  instruction waits in ID/EX; EX/MEM receives bubbles until the result is\n  registered. All RV32I ops stay single-cycle, so the EX loop becomes\n  forward-mux -> 32-bit add/shift/compare -> EX/MEM. Expected Fmax 55-90 MHz\n  (vs 5.44), fitness roughly 120-190 iter/s, i.e. far beyond the +50% the\n  schema field can express.\n\n  Design contract for the implementer:\n  1. muldiv.sv, ports: clock, reset, start, op, a, b -> busy/done, result.\n     - Divider: radix-2 restoring, 1 bit/cycle on magnitudes (setup cycle\n       takes |a|,|b| and records signs; 32 iterate cycles of a 33-bit\n       subtract + shift; final cycle applies sign fix into result_q).\n       RV32M corner cases fall out of the algorithm with one rule: negate\n       the quotient only if signs differ AND b != 0 (div-by-0 -> all ones,\n       rem-by-0 -> dividend, INT_MIN/-1 -> INT_MIN, rem 0). No early-out\n       needed: divides are not on CoreMark's timed path.\n     - Multiplier: ONE 33x33 signed multiply (operand sign bits =\n       a[31]&a_signed, b[31]&b_signed) replacing the three 64-bit ones.\n       Operands are registered (DSP input regs), product registered\n       (DSP output reg), result_q = op==MUL ? p[31:0] : p[63:32].\n       Start with 2 extra cycles; cut to 1 extra cycle only if\n       `make timing` shows reg -> DSP -> EX/MEM is not the worst path.\n     - Under `ifdef RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` keep the SAME FSM/handshake but\n       compute the ALTOPS formulas from the captured operands with a fixed\n       2-cycle latency. Required: insn checks are 20-step BMC and liveness\n       allows ~10 cycles between retirements, so a 34-cycle divide cannot\n       retire inside the window. Real latency is covered by cosim + cocotb.\n  2. ex_stage.sv: m_op = in.valid && ctrl.is_muldiv (new decoded ctrl bit so\n     EX does not compare alu_op). start = m_op && FSM idle, operands = the\n     post-forward rs1/rs2. ex_busy = m_op && !done. When done, EX/MEM\n     captures result_q through the existing alu_result mux; the FSM returns\n     to idle on the cycle EX/MEM actually captures (!stall_ex_mem).\n  3. hazard_unit.sv: stall_if |= ex_busy; stall_id |= ex_busy; new output\n     bubble_ex_mem = ex_busy && !dmem_stall (EX/MEM <= '0). dmem_stall keeps\n     priority (EX/MEM holds, M-unit keeps iterating or waits in DONE). An\n     M-op in EX is never a load or branch, so load_use/redirect/flush_id\n     cannot fire against it.\n  4. Operand staleness (the classic bug): while the M-op waits in EX its\n     producers drain out of EX/MEM and MEM/WB, so ID/EX.rs?_val would be\n     stale at the done cycle (wrong rvfi_rs?_rdata, and wrong operands if\n     start were late). Fix in id_stage.sv: whenever ID/EX holds (stall &&\n     !flush) write the post-forward rs1/rs2 back into ID/EX.rs1_val/rs2_val.\n     Forwarding is then correct on every held cycle by induction, and\n     EX/MEM.rs1_val/rs2_val are right when the M-op finally moves.\n  5. alu.sv keeps only RV32I ops (M arms deleted in both the ALTOPS and real\n     branches, since both now live in muldiv.sv and must stay consistent).\n  Invariants untouched: decoder legality, rvfi_order (+1 per valid MEM/WB),\n  trap discipline, top-level IO, nret=1.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 50\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/muldiv.sv\n    description: \"New sequential M-extension unit: start/busy/done FSM, radix-2 restoring divider (~34 cycles), single 33x33 signed multiplier with registered operands and product, result_q register; ALTOPS branch with the same handshake and fixed 2-cycle latency.\"\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: \"Remove the combinational MUL*/DIV*/REM* arms (real and ALTOPS); ALU becomes RV32I-only add/sub/logic/compare/shift/LUI.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add ctrl_t.is_muldiv (decoded M-op flag) so EX/hazard logic does not decode alu_op.\"\n  - file: rtl/decoder.sv\n    description: \"Set is_muldiv inside the already-validated funct7==0000001 R-type arm only; is_illegal default and legality rules unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Pass is_muldiv into ID/EX; when ID/EX holds (stall && !flush) refresh rs1_val/rs2_val with the post-forward EX operands so a waiting M-op keeps correct operands and RVFI rs*_rdata after its producers retire.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate muldiv, start it on the first EX cycle of an M-op with forwarded operands, mux result_q into EX/MEM.alu_result, export ex_busy and the forwarded rs1/rs2; add bubble input that loads EX/MEM with '0 while busy (hold on dmem stall keeps priority).\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add ex_busy input: OR into stall_if/stall_id, emit bubble_ex_mem = ex_busy && !dmem_stall.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire ex_busy/bubble_ex_mem and the forwarded-operand refresh between ex_stage, id_stage and hazard_unit.\"\n  - file: test/test_muldiv.py\n    description: \"New cocotb suite for muldiv: every M-ext vector currently in test_alu.py ported unchanged (30+ per op, div-by-zero, INT_MIN/-1, back-to-back ops) but driven through the clocked start/done handshake.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Keep the RV32I ALU vectors on the combinational alu; M-ext vectors move verbatim to test_muldiv.py (no vector dropped, invariant 5b coverage preserved).\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add pipeline cases: M-op whose operands are forwarded from EX/MEM and MEM/WB, M-op followed immediately by a consumer, M-op behind a load, back-to-back MUL/DIV; fix any fixed-latency assumptions.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 470, "cache_read_input_tokens": 75602, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 470}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1ef5e113-78fa-45f5-9d31-b22c88a410f8", "uuid": "1d10db3f-c1c3-4a30-a475-fed46e9da409", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:05:06.558Z", "request_id": "req_011ChLceK2TYrGEdRkVtZHve"}
