{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-fable-5-2", "id": "msg_011ChLcWedwZiFoZsgCghoJd", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_019G7Sj18EFUTQvBckdtPAFZ", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-003-r1s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r1s2.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20261123-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-cycle branch prediction: gshare direction + in-cycle pc+imm target, zero-bubble taken branches and JALs\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The core has no branch prediction at all: every taken conditional branch,\n  every JAL and every JALR is resolved in EX and redirects the PC from there,\n  killing the instruction that sits in the merged IF/ID stage (one bubble per\n  redirect). On CoreMark roughly 13-15% of retired instructions redirect\n  (taken branches + calls/jumps/returns), and CPI is only ~1.45-1.5 with the\n  22% bus stalls, so redirect bubbles are ~9% of all counted cycles - the\n  largest IPC loss that is not bus-stall-bound.\n  This core has an unusual property that makes prediction nearly free: there\n  is NO IF/ID register. The fetched instruction word (io_imemData) is visible\n  combinationally in the same cycle the PC addresses it. So the branch/JAL\n  target can be computed from the instruction itself (pc + B/J-immediate) in\n  the fetch cycle and steered straight into the PC register - no BTB, no\n  target misprediction, and a correctly predicted taken branch costs ZERO\n  bubbles. Only the direction needs predicting.\n  Fmax (5.4 MHz) is set by the 200 ns combinational divider path in EX, so\n  the new fetch-cycle path (imemData -> imm mux -> 32-bit add -> PC mux) and\n  the EX verify logic are an order of magnitude away from the critical path;\n  the gain is pure iterations/cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a decode-time (fetch-cycle) branch predictor to the merged IF/ID stage\n  and turn the EX redirect into a misprediction-only redirect.\n\n  1. Pre-decode in if_stage (directly from imem_data, not the NOP-muxed\n     out.instr): is_branch (opcode 1100011), is_jal (1101111). Compute\n     pred_target = pc + (is_jal ? J-imm : B-imm) with one dedicated adder.\n  2. Direction: new branch_predictor.sv - 256-entry table of 2-bit saturating\n     counters, gshare-indexed (pc[9:2] ^ 8-bit global history), async read,\n     sync write. JAL is always predicted taken. pred_taken =\n     fetch_valid && target_aligned && (is_jal || (is_branch && ctr[1])).\n     Never predict taken when the target is misaligned (imm[1]=1) so the\n     existing misalign-trap behaviour (no redirect, pc_next = pc+4) is\n     untouched.\n  3. PC update: redirect (from EX) still has top priority; else if !stall,\n     pc <= pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. The prediction only takes effect\n     in the cycle the instruction actually advances into ID/EX (imem ready,\n     no load-use / dmem stall, no redirect), so pred_taken latched into ID/EX\n     always matches what the PC really did.\n  4. Carry pred_taken (+ the 8-bit table index used) through if_id_t/id_ex_t.\n     In EX: for branches/JAL, actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) &&\n     !misalign_fault; redirect = valid && (actual_taken != pred_taken) (JALR\n     keeps redirecting always, as today, unless predicted by the RAS below);\n     redirect_target = actual_taken ? (jump/branch target) : pc+4.\n     pc_next / RVFI pc_wdata logic is unchanged (always the architecturally\n     correct next PC), so every prediction is verified and any predictor\n     state is functionally safe (formal-friendly: no reset-value dependence).\n  5. Training: when a valid conditional branch leaves EX (not held by a dmem\n     stall) update its counter at the carried index and shift the actual\n     outcome into the global history register. Because only ONE instruction\n     is ever speculative (the one in IF/ID while its predecessor is in EX),\n     an instruction that advances into ID/EX is always on the committed path\n     - no history/RAS repair logic is needed.\n  6. Return-address stack (same module, second priority, drop if it\n     endangers formal/timing): 4-entry RAS pushed with pc+4 when a JAL/JALR\n     with rd in {x1,x5} advances into ID/EX, popped when JALR with rs1 in\n     {x1,x5}, rd=x0 advances. Predicted returns carry a 30-bit pred_target in\n     ID/EX; EX redirects only if jalr target != pred_target. Push/pop are\n     gated by the same \"advances into ID/EX\" condition, so the RAS is never\n     corrupted by wrong-path instructions.\n\n  Expected effect: taken branches predicted correctly and all JALs cost 0\n  bubbles instead of 1; mispredicted branches (either direction) cost 1\n  bubble (same as a taken branch today). With ~90% gshare accuracy the\n  redirect rate drops from ~14% of instructions to ~2-3%, removing ~0.11\n  cycles/instruction from a CPI of ~1.45-1.5: about +7-9% CoreMark\n  iterations/cycle at unchanged Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"New module: 256x2-bit gshare counter table (async read at fetch, sync update from EX), 8-bit global history register updated with resolved outcomes, and a 4-entry return-address stack with push/pop gated by instruction-advance. All state is prediction-only (verified in EX), so any contents are functionally safe.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add pred_taken, pred_idx[7:0] and pred_target[31:2] (RAS-predicted returns) to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Pre-decode imem_data for BRANCH/JAL/return, compute pred_target = pc + B/J-imm in the fetch cycle, next PC = redirect ? redirect_target : (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4). Gate pred_taken with fetch-valid (no flush/redirect/imem stall) and target alignment; emit pred fields in the IF/ID bundle.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch pred_taken / pred_idx / pred_target into the ID/EX register (cleared on flush with the rest of the bundle).\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Redirect only on misprediction: compare actual next-PC decision (branch_taken / jump, minus misalign fault) against pred_taken (and jalr target against pred_target for RAS-predicted returns); redirect_target = actual taken ? target : pc+4. Export branch-resolve training signals (valid conditional branch, taken, pred_idx). pc_next / trap logic unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate branch_predictor, wire fetch-side lookup (pc, advance strobe, call/return pre-decode) and EX-side training (gated by !stall_ex_mem so a held branch trains once).\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"No functional change expected (redirect still means 'EX kills the IF/ID instruction'); touch only if the advance strobe for predictor/RAS updates is exported from here (imem_ready && !load_use && !dmem_stall && !redirect).\"\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: \"cocotb unit test for branch_predictor: counter saturation/training, gshare index/history update, RAS push/pop ordering and gating, and that predictions never assert for non-advancing fetches.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5527, "cache_read_input_tokens": 51074, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 5527}, "output_tokens": 10, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e2b7c249-c955-42c6-95dd-85292c9d85b9", "uuid": "b55d1d48-231a-4e7a-a4c4-fff77db28726", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:03:19.827Z", "request_id": "req_011ChLcWeWkgugNx9KcwbxSL"}
