{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-fable-5-2", "id": "msg_011ChLcjZCNuXNzhtzxuXT1Z", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_011V8M224XXpVsXJJTcSTCrr", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "/srv/hwebench/clones/claude-fable-5_2_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261123-003-r1s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261123-003-r1s2.yaml", "content": "id: hyp-20261123-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-cycle branch prediction: bimodal direction + in-cycle pc+imm target, zero-bubble taken branches and JALs\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The core has no branch prediction: every taken conditional branch, JAL and\n  JALR is resolved in EX and redirects the PC from there, killing the\n  instruction sitting in the merged IF/ID stage (one bubble per redirect).\n  Measured on this RTL (Verilator cosim, CoreMark with --istall --dstall,\n  first 3.0M cycles / 1.95M retirements, RVFI trace):\n    CPI = 1.535; conditional branches = 18.1% of instructions, 55.6% taken;\n    JAL = 1.69%, JALR = 0.47%.\n    Redirects = 12.2% of instructions = 7.96% of ALL cycles are redirect\n    bubbles.\n  This core has a property that makes prediction nearly free: there is NO\n  IF/ID register. The fetched word (io_imemData) is visible combinationally\n  in the same cycle the PC addresses it, so the branch/JAL target can be\n  computed from the instruction itself (pc + B/J-immediate) in the fetch\n  cycle and steered straight into the PC register. No BTB, no target\n  misprediction, and a correctly predicted taken branch costs ZERO bubbles.\n  Only the direction needs predicting.\n  Fmax (5.4 MHz) is set by the ~200 ns combinational divider path in EX, so\n  the new fetch-cycle path (imemData -> imm mux -> 32-bit add -> PC mux) and\n  the 1-LUT change to the EX redirect are an order of magnitude off the\n  critical path; the gain is pure iterations/cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a decode-time (fetch-cycle) branch predictor to the merged IF/ID stage\n  and turn the EX redirect into a misprediction-only redirect.\n\n  1. Pre-decode in if_stage directly from imem_data: is_branch (opcode\n     1100011), is_jal (1101111). Compute pred_target = pc + (is_jal ? J-imm :\n     B-imm) with one dedicated 32-bit adder.\n  2. Direction: new branch_predictor.sv - 256-entry table of 2-bit saturating\n     counters indexed by pc[9:2] (bimodal), async read at fetch, sync write\n     from EX, no reset on the array (LUT-RAM friendly). JAL is always\n     predicted taken.\n       pred_taken = !(flush || redirect) && !imm[1]\n                    && (is_jal || (is_branch && ctr[1]))\n     Never predict taken when the target would be misaligned (imm[1]=1), so\n     the existing misalign-trap behaviour (no redirect, pc_next = pc+4) is\n     untouched.\n  3. PC update: EX redirect keeps top priority; else if !stall,\n     pc <= pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. pred_taken rides in the IF/ID\n     bundle and is latched into ID/EX under the existing stall/flush rules,\n     so the bit in ID/EX always equals what the PC really did (imem stall ->\n     bundle is a NOP with pred_taken=0; load-use -> ID/EX flushed and PC\n     held; dmem stall -> both held; redirect -> ID/EX flushed).\n  4. EX verify: actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) && !misalign_fault.\n     redirect = (actual_taken != pred_taken)   (JALR is never predicted, so\n     it redirects exactly as today);\n     redirect_target = actual_taken ? (is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target)\n                                    : pc + 4.\n     pc_next / trap / link-value logic is unchanged (always the\n     architecturally correct next PC), so every prediction is verified and\n     ANY predictor contents are functionally safe - formal needs no\n     assumptions about table state, and riscv-formal pc_fwd/pc_bwd act as the\n     safety net for the steering logic.\n  5. Training: when a valid conditional branch leaves EX (gated by\n     !stall_ex_mem so a branch held by a dmem stall trains once), update the\n     counter at in.pc[9:2] with the resolved direction. Only one instruction\n     is ever speculative (the one in IF/ID while its predecessor is in EX),\n     so no repair/checkpoint logic exists anywhere.\n\n  Trace-driven evaluation of exactly this scheme on the measured CoreMark\n  RVFI trace (counters initialised weakly-not-taken):\n    static BTFN            20.6% mispredict -> +5.5% iterations/cycle\n    bimodal 64             9.9%             -> +6.9%\n    bimodal 256 (proposed) 8.45%            -> +7.1%\n    bimodal 1024           7.6%             -> +7.25%\n    gshare 256 (h=4..8)    8.8-10.7%        -> +6.8..7.1% (worse: aliasing)\n    gshare 4096 h=12       6.4%             -> +7.4%\n    + 4-entry RAS          99.9% of returns -> only +0.35% extra\n  Almost all of the gain comes from the zero-bubble in-cycle target, not from\n  predictor sophistication, so the proposal deliberately takes the simplest\n  point: bimodal-256, no global history, no RAS (rejected: +0.35% does not\n  justify a 30-bit predicted-target field in ID/EX plus a jalr-adder ->\n  comparator -> redirect path). Redirect bubbles drop from 12.2% to ~2.0% of\n  instructions (8.45% of branches + all JALRs): cycles per iteration fall\n  ~6.7%, i.e. about +7% CoreMark iter/s at unchanged Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"New module: parameterised (IDX_BITS=8) table of 2-bit saturating counters. Async read port indexed by fetch pc[9:2] -> pred bit = ctr[1]; sync update port (en, pc index, taken) driven from EX. No reset on the memory array so Gowin infers LUT-RAM; contents are prediction-only and always verified in EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a pred_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Pre-decode imem_data for BRANCH/JAL, compute pred_target = pc + B/J-imm in the fetch cycle, pred_taken = !(flush||redirect) && !imm[1] && (is_jal || (is_branch && counter MSB)). PC: redirect ? redirect_target : (!stall ? (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4) : hold). Emit pred_taken in the IF/ID bundle; expose pc[9:2] lookup to the predictor.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch in.pred_taken into the ID/EX register (cleared on flush with the rest of the bundle, held on stall).\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Misprediction-only redirect: actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) && !misalign_fault; redirect = actual_taken != in.pred_taken; redirect_target = actual_taken ? (is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target) : in.pc + 4. Export training strobe (in.valid && is_branch), resolved direction and in.pc[9:2]. pc_next, trap and EX/MEM payload logic unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate branch_predictor; wire the fetch-side lookup from if_stage and the EX-side training port gated with !stall_ex_mem so a branch held by a dmem stall updates its counter exactly once.\"\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: \"cocotb unit test for branch_predictor: counter increments/decrements and saturates at 0 and 3, prediction bit follows ctr[1], distinct pc indices are independent, no update when the enable is low.\"\n"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 3191, "cache_read_input_tokens": 77050, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 3191}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e2b7c249-c955-42c6-95dd-85292c9d85b9", "uuid": "528fb396-0f75-46a6-9150-23eedde09c26", "timestamp": "2026-11-23T22:05:29.840Z", "request_id": "req_011ChLcjYrneRen9npe62gXc"}
