al direction + in-cycle pc+imm target, zero-bubble taken branches and JALs\"", " category: predictor", " motivation: |", "-  The core has no branch prediction at all: every taken conditional branch,", "-  every JAL and every JALR is resolved in EX and redirects the PC from there,", "-  killing the instruction that sits in the merged IF/ID stage (one bubble per", "-  redirect). On CoreMark roughly 13-15% of retired instructions redirect", "-  (taken branches + calls/jumps/returns), and CPI is only ~1.45-1.5 with the", "-  22% bus stalls, so redirect bubbles are ~9% of all counted cycles - the", "-  largest IPC loss that is not bus-stall-bound.", "-  This core has an unusual property that makes prediction nearly free: there", "-  is NO IF/ID register. The fetched instruction word (io_imemData) is visible", "-  combinationally in the same cycle the PC addresses it. So the branch/JAL", "-  target can be computed from the instruction itself (pc + B/J-immediate) in", "-  the fetch cycle and steered straight into the PC register - no BTB, no", "-  target misprediction, and a correctly predicted taken branch costs ZERO", "-  bubbles. Only the direction needs predicting.", "-  Fmax (5.4 MHz) is set by the 200 ns combinational divider path in EX, so", "+  The core has no branch prediction: every taken conditional branch, JAL and", "+  JALR is resolved in EX and redirects the PC from there, killing the", "+  instruction sitting in the merged IF/ID stage (one bubble per redirect).", "+  Measured on this RTL (Verilator cosim, CoreMark with --istall --dstall,", "+  first 3.0M cycles / 1.95M retirements, RVFI trace):", "+    CPI = 1.535; conditional branches = 18.1% of instructions, 55.6% taken;", "+    JAL = 1.69%, JALR = 0.47%.", "+    Redirects = 12.2% of instructions = 7.96% of ALL cycles are redirect", "+    bubbles.", "+  This core has a property that makes prediction nearly free: there is NO", "+  IF/ID register. The fetched word (io_imemData) is visible combinationally", "+  in the same cycle the PC addresses it, so the branch/JAL target can be", "+  computed from the instruction itself (pc + B/J-immediate) in the fetch", "+  cycle and steered straight into the PC register. No BTB, no target", "+  misprediction, and a correctly predicted taken branch costs ZERO bubbles.", "+  Only the direction needs predicting.", "+  Fmax (5.4 MHz) is set by the ~200 ns combinational divider path in EX, so", "   the new fetch-cycle path (imemData -> imm mux -> 32-bit add -> PC mux) and", "-  the EX verify logic are an order of magnitude away from the critical path;", "-  the gain is pure iterations/cycle.", "+  the 1-LUT change to the EX redirect are an order of magnitude off the", "+  critical path; the gain is pure iterations/cycle.", " hypothesis: |", "   Add a decode-time (fetch-cycle) branch predictor to the merged IF/ID stage", "   and turn the EX redirect into a misprediction-only redirect.", " ", "-  1. Pre-decode in if_stage (directly from imem_data, not the NOP-muxed", "-     out.instr): is_branch (opcode 1100011), is_jal (1101111). Compute", "-     pred_target = pc + (is_jal ? J-imm : B-imm) with one dedicated adder.", "+  1. Pre-decode in if_stage directly from imem_data: is_branch (opcode", "+     1100011), is_jal (1101111). Compute pred_target = pc + (is_jal ? J-imm :", "+     B-imm) with one dedicated 32-bit adder.", "   2. Direction: new branch_predictor.sv - 256-entry table of 2-bit saturating", "-     counters, gshare-indexed (pc[9:2] ^ 8-bit global history), async read,", "-     sync write. JAL is always predicted taken. pred_taken =", "-     fetch_valid && target_aligned && (is_jal || (is_branch && ctr[1])).", "-     Never predict taken when the target is misaligned (imm[1]=1) so the", "-     existing misalign-trap behaviour (no redirect, pc_next = pc+4) is", "+     counters indexed by pc[9:2] (bimodal), async read at fetch, sync write", "+     from EX, no reset on the array (LUT-RAM friendly). JAL is always", "+     predicted taken.", "+       pred_taken = !(flush || redirect) && !imm[1]", "+                    && (is_jal || (is_branch && ctr[1]))", "+     Never predict taken when the target would be misaligned (imm[1]=1), so", "+     the existing misalign-trap behaviour (no redirect, pc_next = pc+4) is", "      untouched.", "-  3. PC update: redirect (from EX) still has top priority; else if !stall,", "-     pc <= pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. The prediction only takes effect", "-     in the cycle the instruction actually advances into ID/EX (imem ready,", "-     no load-use / dmem stall, no redirect), so pred_taken latched into ID/EX", "-     always matches what the PC really did.", "-  4. Carry pred_taken (+ the 8-bit table index used) through if_id_t/id_ex_t.", "-     In EX: for branches/JAL, actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) &&", "-     !misalign_fault; redirect = valid && (actual_taken != pred_taken) (JALR", "-     keeps redirecting always, as today, unless predicted by the RAS below);", "-     redirect_target = actual_taken ? (jump/branch target) : pc+4.", "-     pc_next / RVFI pc_wdata logic is unchanged (always the architecturally", "-     correct next PC), so every prediction is verified and any predictor", "-     state is functionally safe (formal-friendly: no reset-value dependence).", "-  5. Training: when a valid conditional branch leaves EX (not held by a dmem", "-     stall) update its counter at the carried index and shift the actual", "-     outcome into the global history register. Because only ONE instruction", "+  3. PC update: EX redirect keeps top priority; else if !stall,", "+     pc <= pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. pred_taken rides in the IF/ID", "+     bundle and is latched into ID/EX under the existing stall/flush rules,", "+     so the bit in ID/EX always equals what the PC really did (imem stall ->", "+     bundle is a NOP with pred_taken=0; load-use -> ID/EX flushed and PC", "+     held; dmem stall -> both held; redirect -> ID/EX flushed).", "+  4. EX verify: actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) && !misalign_fault.", "+     redirect = (actual_taken != pred_taken)   (JALR is never predicted, so", "+     it redirects exactly as today);", "+     redirect_target = actual_taken ? (is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target)", "+                                    : pc + 4.", "+     pc_next / trap / link-value logic is unchanged (always the", "+     architecturally correct next PC), so every prediction is verified and", "+     ANY predictor contents are functionally safe - formal needs no", "+     assumptions about table state, and riscv-formal pc_fwd/pc_bwd act as the", "+     safety net for the steering logic.", "+  5. Training: when a valid conditional branch leaves EX (gated by", "+     !stall_ex_mem so a branch held by a dmem stall trains once), update the", "+     counter at in.pc[9:2] with the resolved direction. Only one instruction", "      is ever speculative (the one in IF/ID while its predecessor is in EX),", "-     an instruction that advances into ID/EX is always on the committed path", "-     - no history/RAS repair logic is needed.", "-  6. Return-address stack (same module, second priority, drop if it", "-     endangers formal/timing): 4-entry RAS pushed with pc+4 when a JAL/JALR", "-     with rd in {x1,x5} advances into ID/EX, popped when JALR with rs1 in", "-     {x1,x5}, rd=x0 advances. Predicted returns carry a 30-bit pred_target in", "-     ID/EX; EX redirects only if jalr target != pred_target. Push/pop are", "-     gated by the same \"advances into ID/EX\" condition, so the RAS is never", "-     corrupted by wrong-path instructions.", "+     so no repair/checkpoint logic exists anywhere.", " ", "-  Expected effect: taken branches predicted correctly and all JALs cost 0", "-  bubbles instead of 1; mispredicted branches (either direction) cost 1", "-  bubble (same as a taken branch today). With ~90% gshare accuracy the", "-  redirect rate drops from ~14% of instructions to ~2-3%, removing ~0.11", "-  cycles/instruction from a CPI of ~1.45-1.5: about +7-9% CoreMark", "-  iterations/cycle at unchanged Fmax.", "+  Trace-driven evaluation of exactly this scheme on the measured CoreMark", "+  RVFI trace (counters initialised weakly-not-taken):", "+    static BTFN            20.6% mispredict -> +5.5% iterations/cycle", "+    bimodal 64             9.9%             -> +6.9%", "+    bimodal 256 (proposed) 8.45%            -> +7.1%", "+    bimodal 1024           7.6%             -> +7.25%", "+    gshare 256 (h=4..8)    8.8-10.7%        -> +6.8..7.1% (worse: aliasing)", "+    gshare 4096 h=12       6.4%             -> +7.4%", "+    + 4-entry RAS          99.9% of returns -> only +0.35% extra", "+  Almost all of the gain comes from the zero-bubble in-cycle target, not from", "+  predictor sophistication, so the proposal deliberately takes the simplest", "+  point: bimodal-256, no global history, no RAS (rejected: +0.35% does not", "+  justify a 30-bit predicted-target field in ID/EX plus a jalr-adder ->", "+  comparator -> redirect path). Redirect bubbles drop from 12.2% to ~2.0% of", "+  instructions (8.45% of branches + all JALRs): cycles per iteration fall", "+  ~6.7%, i.e. about +7% CoreMark iter/s at unchanged Fmax.", " expected_impact:", "-  fitness_delta_pct: 8", "+  fitness_delta_pct: 7", "   confidence: medium", " changes:", "   - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv", "-    description: \"New module: 256x2-bit gshare counter table (async read at fetch, sync update from EX), 8-bit global history register updated with resolved outcomes, and a 4-entry return-address stack with push/pop gated by instruction-advance. All state is prediction-only (verified in EX), so any contents are functionally safe.\"", "+    description: \"New module: parameterised (IDX_BITS=8) table of 2-bit saturating counters. Async read port indexed by fetch pc[9:2] -> pred bit = ctr[1]; sync update port (en, pc index, taken) driven from EX. No reset on the memory array so Gowin infers LUT-RAM; contents are prediction-only and always verified in EX.\"", "   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv", "-    description: \"Add pred_taken, pred_idx[7:0] and pred_target[31:2] (RAS-predicted returns) to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\"", "+    description: \"Add a pred_taken bit to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\"", "   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv", "-    description: \"Pre-decode imem_data for BRANCH/JAL/return, compute pred_target = pc + B/J-imm in the fetch cycle, next PC = redirect ? redirect_target : (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4). Gate pred_taken with fetch-valid (no flush/redirect/imem stall) and target alignment; emit pred fields in the IF/ID bundle.\"", "+    description: \"Pre-decode imem_data for BRANCH/JAL, compute pred_target = pc + B/J-imm in the fetch cycle, pred_taken = !(flush||redirect) && !imm[1] && (is_jal || (is_branch && counter MSB)). PC: redirect ? redirect_target : (!stall ? (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4) : hold). Emit pred_taken in the IF/ID bundle; expose pc[9:2] lookup to the predictor.\"", "   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv", "-    description: \"Latch pred_taken / pred_idx / pred_target into the ID/EX register (cleared on flush with the rest of the bundle).\"", "+    description: \"Latch in.pred_taken into the ID/EX register (cleared on flush with the rest of the bundle, held on stall).\"", "   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv", "-    description: \"Redirect only on misprediction: compare actual next-PC decision (branch_taken / jump, minus misalign fault) against pred_taken (and jalr target against pred_target for RAS-predicted returns); redirect_target = actual taken ? target : pc+4. Export branch-resolve training signals (valid conditional branch, taken, pred_idx). pc_next / trap logic unchanged.\"", "+    description: \"Misprediction-only redirect: actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) && !misalign_fault; redirect = actual_taken != in.pred_taken; redirect_target = actual_taken ? (is_jump ? jump_target : branch_target) : in.pc + 4. Export training strobe (in.valid && is_branch), resolved direction and in.pc[9:2]. pc_next, trap and EX/MEM payload logic unchanged.\"", "   - file: rtl/core.sv", "-    description: \"Instantiate branch_predictor, wire fetch-side lookup (pc, advance strobe, call/return pre-decode) and EX-side training (gated by !stall_ex_mem so a held branch trains once).\"", "-  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv", "-    description: \"No functional change expected (redirect still means 'EX kills the IF/ID instruction'); touch only if the advance strobe for predictor/RAS updates is exported from here (imem_ready && !load_use && !dmem_stall && !redirect).\"", "+    description: \"Instantiate branch_predictor; wire the fetch-side lookup from if_stage and the EX-side training port gated with !stall_ex_mem so a branch held by a dmem stall updates its counter exactly once.\"", "   - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py", "-    description: \"cocotb unit test for branch_predictor: counter saturation/training, gshare index/history update, RAS push/pop ordering and gating, and that predictions never assert for non-advancing fetches.\"", "+    description: \"cocotb unit test for branch_predictor: counter increments/decrements and saturates at 0 and 3, prediction bit follows ctr[1], distinct pc indices are independent, no update when the enable is low.\""]}], "originalFile": "id: hyp-20261123-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Fetch-cycle branch prediction: gshare direction + in-cycle pc+imm target, zero-bubble taken branches and JALs\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The core has no branch prediction at all: every taken conditional branch,\n  every JAL and every JALR is resolved in EX and redirects the PC from there,\n  killing the instruction that sits in the merged IF/ID stage (one bubble per\n  redirect). On CoreMark roughly 13-15% of retired instructions redirect\n  (taken branches + calls/jumps/returns), and CPI is only ~1.45-1.5 with the\n  22% bus stalls, so redirect bubbles are ~9% of all counted cycles - the\n  largest IPC loss that is not bus-stall-bound.\n  This core has an unusual property that makes prediction nearly free: there\n  is NO IF/ID register. The fetched instruction word (io_imemData) is visible\n  combinationally in the same cycle the PC addresses it. So the branch/JAL\n  target can be computed from the instruction itself (pc + B/J-immediate) in\n  the fetch cycle and steered straight into the PC register - no BTB, no\n  target misprediction, and a correctly predicted taken branch costs ZERO\n  bubbles. Only the direction needs predicting.\n  Fmax (5.4 MHz) is set by the 200 ns combinational divider path in EX, so\n  the new fetch-cycle path (imemData -> imm mux -> 32-bit add -> PC mux) and\n  the EX verify logic are an order of magnitude away from the critical path;\n  the gain is pure iterations/cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a decode-time (fetch-cycle) branch predictor to the merged IF/ID stage\n  and turn the EX redirect into a misprediction-only redirect.\n\n  1. Pre-decode in if_stage (directly from imem_data, not the NOP-muxed\n     out.instr): is_branch (opcode 1100011), is_jal (1101111). Compute\n     pred_target = pc + (is_jal ? J-imm : B-imm) with one dedicated adder.\n  2. Direction: new branch_predictor.sv - 256-entry table of 2-bit saturating\n     counters, gshare-indexed (pc[9:2] ^ 8-bit global history), async read,\n     sync write. JAL is always predicted taken. pred_taken =\n     fetch_valid && target_aligned && (is_jal || (is_branch && ctr[1])).\n     Never predict taken when the target is misaligned (imm[1]=1) so the\n     existing misalign-trap behaviour (no redirect, pc_next = pc+4) is\n     untouched.\n  3. PC update: redirect (from EX) still has top priority; else if !stall,\n     pc <= pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4. The prediction only takes effect\n     in the cycle the instruction actually advances into ID/EX (imem ready,\n     no load-use / dmem stall, no redirect), so pred_taken latched into ID/EX\n     always matches what the PC really did.\n  4. Carry pred_taken (+ the 8-bit table index used) through if_id_t/id_ex_t.\n     In EX: for branches/JAL, actual_taken = (branch_taken || is_jump) &&\n     !misalign_fault; redirect = valid && (actual_taken != pred_taken) (JALR\n     keeps redirecting always, as today, unless predicted by the RAS below);\n     redirect_target = actual_taken ? (jump/branch target) : pc+4.\n     pc_next / RVFI pc_wdata logic is unchanged (always the architecturally\n     correct next PC), so every prediction is verified and any predictor\n     state is functionally safe (formal-friendly: no reset-value dependence).\n  5. Training: when a valid conditional branch leaves EX (not held by a dmem\n     stall) update its counter at the carried index and shift the actual\n     outcome into the global history register. Because only ONE instruction\n     is ever speculative (the one in IF/ID while its predecessor is in EX),\n     an instruction that advances into ID/EX is always on the committed path\n     - no history/RAS repair logic is needed.\n  6. Return-address stack (same module, second priority, drop if it\n     endangers formal/timing): 4-entry RAS pushed with pc+4 when a JAL/JALR\n     with rd in {x1,x5} advances into ID/EX, popped when JALR with rs1 in\n     {x1,x5}, rd=x0 advances. Predicted returns carry a 30-bit pred_target in\n     ID/EX; EX redirects only if jalr target != pred_target. Push/pop are\n     gated by the same \"advances into ID/EX\" condition, so the RAS is never\n     corrupted by wrong-path instructions.\n\n  Expected effect: taken branches predicted correctly and all JALs cost 0\n  bubbles instead of 1; mispredicted branches (either direction) cost 1\n  bubble (same as a taken branch today). With ~90% gshare accuracy the\n  redirect rate drops from ~14% of instructions to ~2-3%, removing ~0.11\n  cycles/instruction from a CPI of ~1.45-1.5: about +7-9% CoreMark\n  iterations/cycle at unchanged Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"New module: 256x2-bit gshare counter table (async read at fetch, sync update from EX), 8-bit global history register updated with resolved outcomes, and a 4-entry return-address stack with push/pop gated by instruction-advance. All state is prediction-only (verified in EX), so any contents are functionally safe.\"\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add pred_taken, pred_idx[7:0] and pred_target[31:2] (RAS-predicted returns) to if_id_t and id_ex_t.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Pre-decode imem_data for BRANCH/JAL/return, compute pred_target = pc + B/J-imm in the fetch cycle, next PC = redirect ? redirect_target : (pred_taken ? pred_target : pc+4). Gate pred_taken with fetch-valid (no flush/redirect/imem stall) and target alignment; emit pred fields in the IF/ID bundle.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch pred_taken / pred_idx / pred_target into the ID/EX register (cleared on flush with the rest of the bundle).\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Redirect only on misprediction: compare actual next-PC decision (branch_taken / jump, minus misalign fault) against pred_taken (and jalr target against pred_target for RAS-predicted returns); redirect_target = actual taken ? target : pc+4. Export branch-resolve training signals (valid conditional branch, taken, pred_idx). pc_next / trap logic unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Instantiate branch_predictor, wire fetch-side lookup (pc, advance strobe, call/return pre-decode) and EX-side training (gated by !stall_ex_mem so a held branch trains once).\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"No functional change expected (redirect still means 'EX kills the IF/ID instruction'); touch only if the advance strobe for predictor/RAS updates is exported from here (imem_ready && !load_use && !dmem_stall && !redirect).\"\n  - file: test/test_branch_predictor.py\n    description: \"cocotb unit test for branch_predictor: counter saturation/training, gshare index/history update, RAS push/pop ordering and gating, and that predictions never assert for non-advancing fetches.\"\n", "userModified": false}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_011V8M224XXpVsXJJTcSTCrr", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "mode"}}]}
